shape theory.completeness : theory.landscape, theory.coherence.equivalence, theory.branching.canopy {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Section 19 (Persistence Completeness).
//
// The structure/character distinction (from Section 3):
//   Structural claim: asserts coherent structure exists or persistence
//     requires a property. About what kinds of shapes persist.
//   Character claim: asserts specific fact about character of a
//     specific shape. About what specific shapes do.
//
// Theorem 19.3 (Structural Completeness): persistence structure is
// complete about structure. Every structural claim following from
// the axiom is derivable. Three conditions: non-vacuity, necessity
// and sufficiency of the Laws, and saturation (Landscape Completeness).
//
// Theorem 19.3a (Full Completeness): structural completeness IS full
// completeness. The structure/character boundary is scope-dependent:
// what is character at one scope becomes structure at a larger scope.
// The canopy provides the maximal scope.
//
// Theorem 19.4 (Persistence Interpretation of Incompleteness):
// Godel's theorem identifies the structure/character distinction
// within formal systems. "True but unprovable" sentences are
// character claims that the structure admits but does not determine.
//
// Theorem 19.4b (Trace-Canopy Boundary): no single trace through
// a self-referentially rich canopy can derive the character of an
// arbitrary trace without enumeration. The diagonal construction.
//
// Godel's preconditions are not met: the theory's complete set of
// consequences is enumerable only by persistence itself. No Turing
// machine enumerates the canopy. Persistence enumerates itself.
//
// Incompleteness is a property of subsystems within persistence.
// Persistence itself is complete.
//
// Derives from: theory.landscape, theory.coherence.equivalence,
//               theory.branching.canopy
  """
}
